    "media:imagetext": "Later - Mondsy Morning, September 22nd.\nYou remember the irishman who described the weather by\nsaying, \"If you con see yonder hill it is going to rain.\nIf you\ncan't see it, it's raining.\"\nThat's what\nwe feared\nwhen we went\nto bed Saturday night. You can imagine our joy Sunday morning\nwhen we arose early to see the sun shinging brightly and not s\ncloud in the sky. Our train ran along the Lake for \u0026 little way\nand then headed up a valley where we soon changed to another with\ngears which followed a steel gear-way between the tracks.\nThen we\nstarted up - and I mean up. After a couple of hours we changed agein\nto a smaller lighter car and now above the timberline we moved more\nslowly as well as steeply with the most/remendous scenery all around\nus.\nPresently we entered a tunnel carved out of the solid rock\nand for 45 minutes we stayed in that tunnel moving always ever higher\nuntil we arrived at a spot some il,000 feet high. There against a\nclear blue Arizona sky was the Jungfrau peak surrounded by other\nmountoins interspersed with glaciers, and everywhere deep, deep\nsnow.\nIt was o sight from a site which we shall never forget and\nweren't we lucky to have such a perfect dey! This morning as I write\nin our hotel room it is raining hard and the visibility is perhaps\na quarter of a mile with a 3 to 400 foot ceiling.\nWe're leaving\nat 10:30 for Lucerne and I'll carry on from there.\nLater - Lucerne\nLovely trip over the mounteirs and along the shores of\nsmall lakes. Switzerland looks like a well-keit golf course without\nbunkers or rough.\nsinile.\nThere is seemingly\nno problem of rags and riches.\nThere must be lots of rich people\nbut they have the good taste not to swagger.\nThere must be lots\nof poor people but we haven't seen any evidence of poverty.\nLake Lucerne is beautiful - something like Lake George\nexcept thet the mountains are rough and rugged - more like a 10,000'\nShelving Rock with snow perpetuslly on the top. I'll still take\nTopside and Lake George.\nLater - Monday Evening, late\nWe loved Lucerne.\nIt is quaint and different.\nhe arrived\nin time for lunch then drove around the city and out into the\ncountry.\nThen we walked around the old section with its narrow\nstreets, little squares and walls with pictures painted on them.\nWe saw the Lion of Lucerne.\nAfter dinner Charlie Sendmeyer, PAA\nDistrict Sales Manager at Zurich who commutes there from here,\ncame by to see us.\nHe asked us to have a drink at the Old Swiss\nHouse, and of course we readily egreed.\nMr. Buholzer - Gussie\nBusch's f ther-in-law, showed us around the family living quarters\nabove the restaurant.\nShades of Dr. Sayman! Or the Buschs- take\nyour choice.\nWe entered through an old convent l6th century gate\nand from then on it was one museum\npiece after another.\nOne, a\nhuge tile stove with a circular set of steps leading to a single\nchair - also\ntile - where one could sit warmly if not hot. In their",
